    "md5": "90334bc13b5a58558205fc385c707621",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2002,
    "chunk_text": "Port City Colombo joins Western Province\u2019s \u2018Lights of Hope\u2019 amid national recovery\n\nPort City Colombo marked the start of the festive season with its annual Christmas Tree Lighting Ceremony, held under the theme \u201cLights of Hope,\u201d in alignment with the Western Province\u2019s initiative. As Sri Lanka continues to recover from the impact of Cyclone Ditwah, the ceremony was more than a seasonal observance; it offered a moment of reflection and unity, bringing together leaders and stakeholders in support of national recovery efforts.\n\nThe event was attended by senior government officials, dignitaries, partners, and stakeholders, with Hon. Hanif Yusoof, Governor of the Western Province, gracing the occasion as Chief Guest. In his address, the Governor underscored the importance of collective responsibility and standing together as communities rebuild. He also noted with appreciation CHEC Port City Colombo (Pvt) Ltd.\u2019s Rs. 10 million contribution to the Government\u2019s \u2018Rebuilding Sri Lanka\u2019 Fund and the deployment of heavy machinery and technical support along the A5 route from Kandy to Nuwara Eliya, assisting disaster-response and infrastructure restoration efforts.\n\nSpeaking at the event, Harsha Amarasekera (PC), Chairman of the Colombo Port City Economic Commission, highlighted that the ceremony went beyond a festive tradition. He said aligning with the Western Province\u2019s \u201cLights of Hope\u201d initiative reflects Port City Colombo\u2019s commitment to responsible engagement and solidarity during challenging times. He also praised the strong partnership between the Commission, the Government, and CHEC Port City Colombo (Pvt) Ltd., noting the company\u2019s swift response to support disaster relief and infrastructure recovery.\n\nA poignant highlight of the ceremony was a one-minute silence, observed in remembrance, prayer, and solidarity with those affected by Cyclone Ditwah. The moment served as a reminder of shared loss, strength, and collective hope for recovery."
