    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0543d770d564b32b9d74c477f011f21f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1966,
    "chunk_text": "Why have the authorities failed in this matter, requiring the Supreme Court intervention? Blame is apportioned on the UGC, university authorities, vice chancellors and the law enforcement authorities. These bodies together constitute a formidable bloc of authority, power and resources. They may have failed to take action or taken it inadequately but where they have been successful in carrying it through, though seldom, subsequently, they were left to face the wrath of the raggers in all its fury more or less alone. Vice chancellors have been under siege in their offices fearing for their lives. UGC similarly attacked, police officers humiliated. Governments too have felt their mutinous rebellion. Universities had to be closed disrupting the studies of innocent students who were the majority. Moreover, police presence was not tolerated by the students and would invariably lead to confrontation which the authorities wanted to avoid. This resulted in a situation where police protection was not readily available to the VCs when under threat.\n\nThe VCs and staff had to live among murderers knowing that police intervention may not be available in an emergency. The governments too did not want the ragging incidents to grow into student rebellion that could be a threat to its own existence. Thus, nobody wanted to take stern action to clamp down on ragging and take the culprits to task. An attempt to do so had the danger of causing a much wider and violent uprising of students involving all the universities. Everybody preferred to soft peddle. Though the UGC had issued clear guidelines these were seldom followed. For instance, though anti-rag committees were appointed as instructed by the UGC these were ineffective in the main. However, university authorities cannot be blamed for all this for the reasons mentioned above. They know that they cannot rely on the higher authorities to back them and take effective action to guarantee their safety."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2025-12-20-islandlk-c0d12cff",
    "num": "islandlk-c0d12cff",
    "date_str": "2025-12-20",
    "description": "Intractability of ragging in universities",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/intractability-of-ragging-in-universities/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1766218740.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-12-20-islandlk-c0d12cff-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "88dba37bb11d2a8b44c6239415620a2d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1886,
    "chunk_text": "The VCs and staff had to live among murderers knowing that police intervention may not be available in an emergency. The governments too did not want the ragging incidents to grow into student rebellion that could be a threat to its own existence. Thus, nobody wanted to take stern action to clamp down on ragging and take the culprits to task. An attempt to do so had the danger of causing a much wider and violent uprising of students involving all the universities. Everybody preferred to soft peddle. Though the UGC had issued clear guidelines these were seldom followed. For instance, though anti-rag committees were appointed as instructed by the UGC these were ineffective in the main. However, university authorities cannot be blamed for all this for the reasons mentioned above. They know that they cannot rely on the higher authorities to back them and take effective action to guarantee their safety.\n\nIt is against this background that we have to see how ragging could be stopped. Ragging must absolutely be treated as a police matter and the government must take steps to ensure that the university authorities are fully backed and their safety ensured by effective police involvement whenever necessary. They must have the freedom and authority to summon the police when law and order is breached or their safety is in the slightest danger. Incidence of ragging must be reported to the police and the police must conduct the investigations in a thorough manner. Victims and witnesses must be given the assurance that there will be no retaliation if they come forward to give evidence.\n\nThis could be done only if retaliation is dealt with sternly and those responsible are taken to task and penalized according to the law. Raggers must be made to feel that they are not out of reach of the legal arm and any action to silence the witnesses would entail greater punishment."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
