    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d7805508a974e42c649ec609993dd729",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1800,
    "chunk_text": "This could be done only if retaliation is dealt with sternly and those responsible are taken to task and penalized according to the law. Raggers must be made to feel that they are not out of reach of the legal arm and any action to silence the witnesses would entail greater punishment.\n\nLegal cases against raggers must be heard and completed promptly and punishment meted out without delay and there should not be sympathy or leniency. Student protests against legal action should not be tolerated and should be clamped down with vigour. Police presence must remain until normalcy returns.\n\nFurthermore, preemptive action could be taken by seeking out the rag leaders and finding evidence about their activities and plans regarding organization of ragging. These activities could be made punishable by law, in the same way that crime is preempted by punishing the plotters. The CID may have to get involved in carrying out this aspect of the project.\n\nAs mentioned at the beginning of this note, the onus is on the government to see that ragging in the universities is completely eradicated. It cannot be done without the greater involvement of the police including the CID in order to ensure the safety of the university authorities, staff and particularly the witnesses and also to target the rag leaders. Without strong government backing and support the universities and the law enforcement authorities alone cannot achieve this goal.\n\nWhy the Proposed Amendments to Sri Lanka\u2019s Universities Act Raise Fears of Authoritarianism, Democratic Backsliding, and a Dangerous Precedent for Academic Freedom\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
