    "chunk_size_bytes": 1643,
    "chunk_text": "Nadesan had an extraordinarily brilliant mind. He had an excellent knack for distilling complex issues into simple propositions applying first principles. He used case law and legal texts to develop them.\n\nSuch was the stature enjoyed by Nadesan that he commanded the confidence of the bench and the admiration of his peers. Esteemed seniors of the Bar, including Dr. Colvin R. de Silva and H. L. de Silva, fondly called him \u201cBoss,\u201d an epithet that captured both their respect for his judgment and their affection for his character.\n\nIn his lectures titled\u00a0The Road to Justice, Lord Denning cited the essential virtue of a lawyer as courage. He highlighted Thomas Erskine\u2019s defence of Thomas Paine as a prime example: an unpopular cause that Lord Loughborough, the Lord Chancellor, explicitly warned Erskine not to take. Erskine accepted the brief anyway, a decision which meant he subsequently had to forego appointment to the prestigious office of Attorney General.\n\nNadesan was clearly made in Erskine\u2019s mould. He would fight any case he believed in, regardless of its unpopularity, always fighting his client\u2019s corner when he believed in the justice of his cause.\n\nDuring the turmoil of\u00a0Black July\u00a0in 1983 the\u00a0Hewamanne v de Silva\u00a0case, which Nadesan had argued, was listed for pronouncement of judgment on Wednesday, July 27. Despite widespread, ongoing violence against Tamils, he insisted on attending court, stating he had never kept judges waiting. When he arrived in Hulftsdorp, no judges were present; they arrived an hour later, clearly after being informed Nadesan was in attendance. I accompanied Nadesan to Court."
