    "md5": "e4fabbd4fd41bf5ac74973bd4d87b728",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1839,
    "chunk_text": "On 9 September 1983, Nadesan was addressing a five-judge Bench of the Supreme Court in a Saturday Review matter when the hearing was abruptly adjourned. It had come to light that the judges had taken their new oaths before one another rather than before the President, apparently contrary to the requirement of the Sixth Amendment. The judges did not turn up in court after the adjournment and the courts and judges\u2019 chambers were locked out, and armed guards barred entry. Confusion followed for several days. Fresh letters of appointment were subsequently issued, and each judge was required to take two new oaths.\n\nThe Deputy Solicitor General argued that the President had inherited the mantle of the monarch, and that Judges owed their allegiance to the President. The Chief Justice in his judgment disagreed with the state\u2019s argument and asserted that the judges swore allegiance to the Constitution. The Court, by a majority, held that the requirement to take the oath before the President was not mandatory but directory. The requirement to take the oath within one month of the date of the coming into force of the sixth Amendment was mandatory but this was complied with and therefore the judges did not cease to hold office.\n\nNadesan had no affiliation with any political party when he was elected to the Senate in 1947. In his maiden speech Nadesan declared that he was an independent senator.\n\nNadesan took his responsibilities as a senator seriously and the speeches he made on the floor of the Senate were incisive and illuminating. The speech he made in the wake of the hartal in 1952 was described by James Manor in his biography of SWRD Bandaranaike as \u201ca devastating assault on the government\u2019s conduct during the hartal and on the draconian public security legislation which it had rushed through parliament\u201d."
