    "chunk_id": "2025-12-22-islandlk-c84b41e3-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d5886e2a58c9737d938d82db27a0454c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "No evidence that babies were used as bait in colonial Sri Lanka, says leading herpetologist\n\nDr. Anslem de Silva debunks sensational colonial-era narratives after comprehensive review\n\nFor more than a century, sensational headlines in foreign newspapers have claimed that British sport hunters in colonial Ceylon used human babies as live bait to lure crocodiles. These chilling tales \u2014 often framed as exotic curiosities \u2014 appeared in 19th and early 20th-century American, British and Australian tabloids, complete with dramatic illustrations of infants tied to riverbanks and European hunters waiting in ambush.\n\nBut according to Sri Lanka\u2019s foremost herpetologist and internationally acclaimed reptile expert Dr. Anslem de Silva, there is no credible historical, archaeological, sociological or biological evidence that such a practice ever took place on the island.\n\nIn a landmark paper published in the Journal of Threatened Taxa, co-authored with crocodile biologist Dr. Ruchira Somaweera, Dr. de Silva painstakingly reviewed the colonial literature, early hunting records, local oral tradition, and extensive scientific surveys. The conclusion is clear: the \u201cbaby-baiting\u201d stories were almost certainly fabricated, exaggerated or recycled sensationalism by foreign presses.\n\n\u201cNot a single reliable record exists in Sri Lankan sources\u201d \u2014 Dr. de Silva\n\nSpeaking to The Island, Dr. de Silva emphasised the striking absence of any supporting evidence from Sri Lankan history:\n\n\u201cDespite thousands of years of written and oral traditions involving crocodiles in Sri Lanka, not a single local source \u2014 not folklore, not hunting journals, not historical documents \u2014 mentions babies being used as bait. The only references come from sensationalist foreign newspapers, most likely self-propagated stories.\u201d\n\nThe research team examined centuries of material, from the Mah\u00e2vamsa to detailed accounts by British, Dutch and Portuguese naturalists and hunters."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
