    "chunk_text": "Colombo Bourse erases early gains as Dockyard plunges and IMF loan concerns dampen sentiment\n\nThe Colombo Stock Exchange (CSE) saw early optimism quickly evaporate yesterday, closing in negative territory as investor sentiment was hit by a perfect storm of company-specific woes and macroeconomic concerns. The market\u2019s retreat was primarily fueled by a continued slide in shares of\u00a0Colombo Dockyard PLC\u00a0and rising apprehension over Sri Lanka\u2019s debt obligations following a new\u00a0USD 200 million IMF emergency loan.\n\nTrading activity, which opened on a positive note, turned decisively as Colombo Dockyard\u2019s stock already in a severe downtrend remained a focal point of investor anxiety. Its decline is linked to the ongoing mechanics of a strategic rights issue for a takeover by India\u2019s Mazagon Dock Shipbuilders. Simultaneously, news of the International Monetary Fund\u2019s rapid financial instrument for climate-related disaster relief, while addressing urgent needs, inadvertently sparked investor fears about the nation\u2019s escalating debt stock, further cooling market participation and pulling indices lower.\n\nAmid those developments both indices moved downward. All Share Price Index down by 229.09 points while S and P SL20 down by 30.95 points. Turnover stood at Rs 2.98 billion with six crossings.\n\nThose crossings were reported in Hemas Holdings, which crossed 10 million shares to the tune of Rs 340 million and its share price traded at Rs 34, JKH 3.1 million shares crossed to the tune of Rs 65.5 million and its share price traded at Rs 21, Tokyo Cement 398,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 44.9 million and its share price traded at Rs 112, Melstacorp 108,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 30.4 million and its share price traded at Rs 169, NTB 100,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 30 million and its share price traded at Rs 300 and Royal Ceramics 500,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 22.5 million and its share price traded at Rs 45."
