    "md5": "397610961531ed4a5daa3a77223a67c1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1348,
    "chunk_text": "T-Bill yields climb as Central Bank calibrates auction\n\nYields on government Treasury Bills rose across the board at this week\u2019s auction, indicating a slight shift in investor sentiment. The Central Bank accepted Rs. 82.5 billion out of a much larger total of bids, with the sharpest increase seen for the 12-month bill.\n\nIn the secondary market, trading was quiet initially but saw heightened selling pressure later in the week, pushing yields higher, particularly for short to medium-term securities.\n\nThe rupee held steady against the US dollar, closing unchanged at Rs. 309.54.\n\nMarket analysts said this uptick in yields likely reflects shifting market expectations, potentially anticipating tighter liquidity conditions or future policy adjustments.\n\n\u201cThe Central Bank\u2019s decision to accept only a portion of the bids, while allowing yields to rise, suggests a calibrated approach to managing borrowing costs. This movement increases the government\u2019s interest expense on new debt, signalling cautious investor sentiment amid evolving macroeconomic factors,\u201d they noted.\n\nSri Lanka to host South Asia\u2019s inaugural Reggae festival in Bentota\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
