    "chunk_text": "While Sanghamitt\u00e2 Mah\u00e2ther\u00ee was selecting her team at the royal palace at P\u00e2aliputra in Jambud\u00eepa, an equally powerful, meticulous, preparation was happening in Sri Lanka.\n\nM\u00e2hesi Anul\u00e2 Dev\u00ee, sister-in-law of King Dev\u00e2napiya Tissa, did not wait passively, twiddling her thumbs.\n\nThe Mah\u00e2va\u1e43sa records that before Sa\u1e45ghamitt\u00e2 Mah\u00e2ther\u00ee even boarded the ship, M\u00e2hesi Anul\u00e2 Dev\u00ee and her entourage of a thousand had already transformed themselves. They did not wait to be told how to be Bhikkhun\u00ees; they instructed themselves. They attended discourses by Mahinda Thera.\n\nThey then discarded their royal finery, shaved their heads, and donned the yellow robes (k\u00e2s\u00e2ya). They moved out of the palace and created their own interim monastery, the Up\u00e2sik\u00e2 Vih\u00e2ra.\n\nPractising the Ten Precepts, wearing the yellow robe, disciplined, they waited for the pabbajj\u00e2 (ordination), looking for the coming of the Theri.\n\nA word for the student or the professional. M\u00e2hesi Anul\u00e2 did not say, \u201cI will become a Bhikkhun\u00ee when the teacher arrives.\u201d She effectively said: \u201cI WILL BECOME A BHIKKHUN\u00ce, SO THE TEACHER ARRIVES.\u201d\n\nIn global marketing strategy, we talk about the \u201cdemand vacuum\u201d. M\u00e2hesi Anul\u00e2 Dev\u00ee created a \u201cdemand vacuum.\u201d Her preparation was intense. An exodus of one thousand women, moving out of royal households into modest living, already wearing robes \u2014 would have exerted a gravitational pull on the mission. This was the urgency that Sa\u1e45ghamitt\u00e2 Mah\u00e2ther\u00ee used to brief the Emperor. She had to go.\n\nWhen the ship finally arrived at Jambukola, it was not a meeting of strangers. It was the coming together of two strategic leaders.\n\nThe experiences of Sanghamitt\u00e2 Mah\u00e2ther\u00ee and Mahes\u00ee Anul\u00e2 Dev\u00ee are not dusty spiritual wall-flowers, bizarre statues, or grotesque artifacts. They are vibrant case studies in strategic foresight."
  },
