    "language": "en",
    "md5": "468595cff7ab173b502cd4e087c52cbe",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1787,
    "chunk_text": "I made this decision on the very first day President Anura Kumara Dissanayake presented the Budget proposals. On that same day, I met Opposition Leader Sajith Premadasa and explained why this move should be welcomed, as it directly benefits the hill country people. Mr. Premadasa understood my position and informed me that I was free to vote with the government on this matter.\n\nQ: How would you describe the foreign policy direction of the NPP government?\n\nIf we look at the Ukraine\u2013Russia conflict, we see the importance of regional geopolitics. Ukraine placed its trust in distant superpowers rather than carefully managing its relationship with its neighboring superpower, and ultimately that support did not materialize when it was most needed.\n\nSri Lanka has assurances from countries like the US and China, but India remains our closest and most reliable neighbor. We must align ourselves more closely with the Indian economy and increase movement between our two countries, particularly to boost tourism. India has consistently been the first to assist us during crises\u2014during the tsunami, the economic crisis, and most recently during Cyclone Ditwah. Strengthening ties with India should therefore be a priority.\n\nQ: What is the current situation of the Tamil Progressive Alliance?\n\nA:We continue to work at the grassroots level. We contested the last election under the SJB ticket, as the SJB is an alliance and the TPA is a member through a Memorandum of Understanding.\n\nThe last election was dominated by a strong public backlash against the old political order, which had become associated with corruption and racism. That wave was so powerful that many prominent politicians, including former ministers, lost their seats, while the NPP secured 159 seats in Parliament."
  },
