    "md5": "20898932b6a64e2888c4d705e2ea3396",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1944,
    "chunk_text": "The last election was dominated by a strong public backlash against the old political order, which had become associated with corruption and racism. That wave was so powerful that many prominent politicians, including former ministers, lost their seats, while the NPP secured 159 seats in Parliament.\n\nQ: The NPP has strong and new representation in the hill country. How do other parties, including yours, compare at the grassroots level?\n\nA:Hill country parties like ours have always fought for the rights and needs of plantation workers. We understand their problems deeply and raise them regardless of political inconvenience.\n\nThe NPP, despite having representation in plantation areas, operates within strict party limits. Their MPs can only raise issues approved by the party leadership and must strictly adhere to party policy. They cannot go beyond that framework.\n\nDuring the Yahapalana period, we fought for land and housing rights for estate workers. I was able to secure seven-perch land plots for 8,000 families. The Indian government funded 4,000 houses, while the Sri Lankan government funded the rest.\n\nThere were also legal barriers preventing Pradeshiya Sabha funds from being used for estate sector development. We fought, even against our own government, to amend those laws and passed the necessary legislation in Parliament. It is doubtful whether the NPP could do the same due to party constraints.\n\nEarlier, there were only two Pradeshiya Sabhas in the Nuwara Eliya\u2013Maskeliya district, forcing people to travel up to 65 kilometers for basic services. During the Yahapalana period, I increased that number to six: Kotagala, Ambewela, Maskeliya, Agarapathana, Norwood, and Nuwara Eliya. We also expanded AGA divisions from two to four and established a New Villages Development Authority for plantation regions. I do not believe such flexible, people-focused development would be possible under the rigid NPP structure."
  },
