    "md5": "b86da4331d2dd27bced90d40c2bb0697",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1969,
    "chunk_text": "Earlier, there were only two Pradeshiya Sabhas in the Nuwara Eliya\u2013Maskeliya district, forcing people to travel up to 65 kilometers for basic services. During the Yahapalana period, I increased that number to six: Kotagala, Ambewela, Maskeliya, Agarapathana, Norwood, and Nuwara Eliya. We also expanded AGA divisions from two to four and established a New Villages Development Authority for plantation regions. I do not believe such flexible, people-focused development would be possible under the rigid NPP structure.\n\nIf this government fails, it will not be because of the President, but because of the ministers. Any involvement in corruption or dishonesty would bring this government to an end.\n\nThis government rose to power largely through social media. While they currently maintain strong control over social media narratives, it is well known that social media tends to favor opposition forces rather than governments. When governing, crises are inevitable, and the same tools once used against former governments are now being used against the NPP.\n\nThe NPP/JVP also made promises they are unable to deliver on. For instance, they claimed petrol could be sold at Rs. 100 per liter if taxes were removed. More than a year has passed, and this has not happened. They criticized previous governments for importing rice, claiming shortages could be predicted and avoided. Have they succeeded in doing so? It appears they are now learning that a country cannot be run solely on ideology.\n\nThere is also a narrative that the Opposition Leader can never become President. Unfortunately, some opposition figures promote this idea for personal gain. Similar claims were once made about M.K. Stalin in Tamil Nadu, yet today he is Chief Minister. Sajith Premadasa faces continuous mudslinging. In the last presidential election, had it not been for Ranil Wickremesinghe dividing the vote between the UNP and SJB, Sajith Premadasa could well have been President today."
  },
