    "chunk_size_bytes": 1564,
    "chunk_text": "The book also explores Sri Lanka\u2019s coastal beauty, including Mirissa, Arugam Bay, Negombo, Hikkaduwa and many more. These beaches are portrayed for the communities and lifestyles that surround them. In a scuba diving experience, authors reflect that, \u201cIt was a meditative experience. The ocean, with infinite mysteries, was open to us. I have never felt peace like that.\u201d Such reflections encourage readers to think about environmental preservation while inspiring them to travel.\n\nPerhaps the most powerful element of the book is its strong focus on people. Throughout the 200 stories, local voices ring out; guides, villagers, artisans, shop owners, farmers, and fellow travelers. It is these meetings of human beings with human beings that give the book its roots. The authors also share insights they have picked up through their guides. For example, under the magnets of \u2018Vesak Day\u2019 and \u2018Poya Day\u2019, they relate how their guide goes to temples, conducts religious rituals, and distributes alms with his family while depicting Buddhist heritage. The book reminds us that travel is ultimately about relationships and that places become meaningful because of the people who inhabit them.\n\nIt is not an easy task to review this book within the limits of a short article as it covers an immense area of Sri Lankan cultural and heritage landmarks. Ultimately, \u2018Around the World in Magnets \u2013 Sri Lanka Edition\u2019, is not a collection of 200 magnets. It is a tribute to Sri Lanka, its people, and its spirit by Dr Antony Vipin Das and Vidushi Duggal."
  },
