    "md5": "229b723466d21b9e5b862999693b22dd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1254,
    "chunk_text": "Cyclone Ditwah & The Future Of Sri Lanka\u2019s Resilience: Rebuilding In An Era Of Escalating Risk\n\nAnother major disaster has resulted in severe loss of life, widespread disruption, and extensive damage across the country, setting Sri Lanka back decades or more. The scale of destruction and the interaction of multiple hazards made emergency response and recovery extraordinarily challenging. Cyclone Ditwah stands among the most devastating events Sri Lanka has faced in recent decades, affecting all 25 districts with extreme rainfall, deadly landslides, and widespread flooding. More than 2 million people were affected, nearly 90,000 homes were damaged or destroyed, and close to 100,000 individuals sought refuge in temporary shelters. As of 11 December 2025, authorities reported a staggering death toll of 639 people and more than 190 missing, predominantly due to landslides and flooding. The central highlands recorded exceptionally intense rainfall, up to 540 mm in 24 hours, triggering numerous significant landslides, while districts such as Puttalam, Gampaha, and Colombo experienced severe riverine and flash flooding. Even with the most advanced preparedness systems, any country would have struggled to contain an event of this magnitude."
  },
