    "md5": "0933184a2304637e9f83893e1425fb56",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1693,
    "chunk_text": "Flooding is a natural feature of Sri Lanka\u2019s river basins, but it has been significantly worsened by deforestation, the disruption of natural floodways, encroachment into floodplains, and unsafe development on landslide-prone slopes. Effective floodplain management such as delineating regulatory flood zones, restricting encroachments, and requiring flood or landslide insurance remains largely absent. Misconceptions about traditional design floods persist. Climate change is expected to increase the frequency of high-magnitude hazards, necessitating rebuilding to higher protection standards than those used today so that, when the next storm arrives, the country is more resilient.\n\nDeforestation: Clearing forests in headwaters river basins accelerates runoff and increases sediment loads that alter river channels.\n\nDisruption of Natural Floodways: Traditional pathways, creeks, streams, wetlands, and paddy fields, have been obstructed by encroaching settlements and poorly designed infrastructure such as roads with drainage.\n\nEncroachment on Floodplains: Expansion of urban development into flood-prone lowlands has increased exposure during high-water events.\n\nUnsafe Housing in Landslide Zones: The construction of homes at the base of unstable slopes has heightened vulnerability during periods of intense rainfall.\n\nGovernance fragmentation further complicates flood and landslide management, with responsibilities distributed across multiple ministries and agencies. Countries with stronger flood-risk outcomes often consolidate these functions under a dedicated authority with clear mandates and technical capacity. Sri Lanka could benefit from similar institutional reforms."
  },
  {
