    "date_str": "2025-12-29",
    "description": "Cyclone Ditwah & The Future Of Sri Lanka\u2019s Resilience: Rebuilding In An Era Of Escalating Risk",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.colombotelegraph.com/index.php/cyclone-ditwah-the-future-of-sri-lankas-resilience-rebuilding-in-an-era-of-escalating-risk/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "colombotelegraphcom",
    "time_ut": 1767013432.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-12-29-colombotelegraphcom-8d9c30c1-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "76e72106ad533a2efa22df09c76c3845",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "Governance fragmentation further complicates flood and landslide management, with responsibilities distributed across multiple ministries and agencies. Countries with stronger flood-risk outcomes often consolidate these functions under a dedicated authority with clear mandates and technical capacity. Sri Lanka could benefit from similar institutional reforms.\n\nTechnological advancements, such as enhanced early warning systems, are equally important. Reports of government interest in acquiring high-resolution Doppler radar for the Meteorology Department are encouraging, as radar provides essential spatial detail on storm events, especially given the limitations of Sri Lanka\u2019s rain-gauge network. Expanding real-time rainfall and river-level monitoring through telemetry or cellular networks and establishing a centralized flood forecasting system based on robust rainfall forecasts, hydrologic models, and flood predictions ahead of storms, is urgently needed. Without a reasonably accurate forecast several days in advance, the NBRO cannot provide actionable landslide risk predictions, and the Irrigation Department will be unable to develop operational strategies for managing floods prior to an advancing storm. The Meteorology Department will need to acquire the necessary tools and expertise to provide such forecasts.\n\nRecent actions by the government and the public demonstrate notable resolve and commitment in responding to this crisis. However, given the scale of the disaster, particularly for a small island nation, recovery will be a demanding and complex process. As climate change is expected to increase both the frequency and intensity of similar events, reconstruction efforts must adopt a forward-looking approach. In this context, recovery should be guided by the principles of \u201cbuilding back better\u201d (B3), designed for future conditions rather than those of the past. Globally, this concept is becoming the norm."
  },
