    "md5": "7c093cb5bcfe4299283331973819c54e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1890,
    "chunk_text": "Savvy political leadership in the present context means saying plainly:\n\nWe will protect low-income travellers \u2013 but we will not subsidise inefficiency forever.\n\nWe will partner with the private sector \u2013 but we will not compromise the public interest.\n\nHow Sri Lanka treats its railway after Ditwah is a proxy for something greater than good governance alone. It is a proof yet to be demonstrated whether the country as a whole is willing to move from reactive crisis management to strategic, sustainable, long-term thinking.\n\nWe cannot keep waiting for disasters to justify reforms we already know are necessary.\n\nWe know the infrastructure was fragile. We know the governance model was outdated. We know climate shocks will intensify. We know rail is indispensable.\n\nSo, the case for change is not technical. It is moral, ethical, and practical.\n\nThis is not utopian. Countries poorer than Sri Lanka have done it. Countries with worse starting points have done it.\n\nThe difference is not money. It is discipline, continuity, and the refusal to treat disaster recovery as a paint job.\n\nCyclone Ditwah has created an unusual alignment: public awareness, donor attention, and political necessity. That alignment will not last.\n\nReform delayed will be reform denied. If we simply patch, clear debris, and declare victory, we will have wasted the most valuable asset a crisis can give: legitimacy for change. One that was promised, and one for which a cardinal opportunity was delivered by fate, karma, chance, destiny, the curse of Kuveni. Take your pick \u2013 and pick up the slack.\n\nDown one track lies the comfortable path: rebuild what we had, amber light blinking, delays baked in, deficits widening, future storms waiting to repeat the trauma.\n\nDown the other lies a harder path: governance reform, climate-proofing, disciplined PPPs, smarter funding, and technological modernisation."
  },
