    "chunk_size_bytes": 1937,
    "chunk_text": "Coastal law rollback could lift Sri Lanka\u2019s risk premium, say experts\n\nAny move to dilute Sri Lanka\u2019s Coast Conservation Law by retroactively legalising illegal coastal developments may increase\u2014not reduce\u2014investment risk, governance analysts and environmental experts warn.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s coastline underpins key sectors including tourism, ports, fisheries, logistics and coastal real estate. It also functions as critical natural infrastructure, absorbing climate shocks and reducing disaster losses. Weakening its legal protection could expose investors to higher climate risk, regulatory uncertainty and ESG-related financing constraints.\n\nEnvironmental scientist and Centre for Environmental Justice (CEJ) chairman Hemantha Withanage told The Island Financial Review that regularising illegal developments through fines signals policy instability.\n\n\u201cMarkets depend on predictable rules. When violations are rewarded, compliant investors are disadvantaged and regulatory credibility is damaged, he added.\n\nThe Coast Conservation Act No. 57 of 1981\u2014strengthened in 2011\u2014clearly defines the coastal zone (300 metres inland, two kilometres seaward) and prohibits development without prior approval. Post-2004 Tsunami buffer zones further restricted construction in high-risk areas. These measures align with global best practice on disaster-risk reduction and climate adaptation.\n\nAccording to Withanage, many coastal assets now under scrutiny were built after the Tsunami, in breach of these safeguards. \u201cLegalising them after construction transfers future disaster risk from developers to the public and financial system, he said.\n\nClimate stress amplifies the concern. Rising sea levels, coastal erosion and stronger storm surges are already affecting Sri Lanka\u2019s shoreline. Projections indicate extensive coastal inundation by 2100, directly impacting asset valuation, insurance availability and long-term returns."
