    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/coastal-law-rollback-could-lift-sri-lankas-risk-premium-say-experts/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1767197940.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-12-31-islandlk-75d21fff-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "86e9ee54b05c2a8b8b30f3fcad28f941",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1365,
    "chunk_text": "Climate stress amplifies the concern. Rising sea levels, coastal erosion and stronger storm surges are already affecting Sri Lanka\u2019s shoreline. Projections indicate extensive coastal inundation by 2100, directly impacting asset valuation, insurance availability and long-term returns.\n\nSections 14 and 15 of the Act explicitly prohibit permits for projects that harm coastal stability or environmental quality. Analysts warn that diluting these provisions could invite litigation, policy reversals and scrutiny from international financing partners.\n\nSelective enforcement also distorts competition. Luxury hotels charging USD 200\u2013300 per night face minimal penalties, while small operators and coastal communities bear compliance costs. This imbalance heightens social risk\u2014an increasingly material factor in investor assessments.\n\nPublic access to beaches is shrinking despite legal mandates to protect it, fuelling community opposition and operational risk. Meanwhile, poorly regulated coastal sand mining has accelerated erosion, undermining long-term asset viability.\n\nBank of Ceylon contributes Rs. 500 million to government\u2019s \u201cRebuilding Sri Lanka\u201d fund\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
